The final part of the answer is operational. After investing, the group helps companies focus, get organized, hire strong talent, and stay focused on the right things: $\mathcal{H}(X)
=
\bigl(
\text{organization},
\text{talent},
\text{focus}
\bigr).$ This is where capital connects back to the rest of the book. A young CEO has too many things coming at once and too few people to handle them. The investor’s role is therefore not only to provide money, but to reduce confusion around what matters most: $\text{capital}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{organization}+\text{hiring}+\text{focus}.$

